{
 "id": "r5-long-vowels",
 "title": "Long vowels",
 "ar": "حُرُوفُ المَدّ",
 "goal": "Tell short vowels from long ones and read words with ā, ī, ū.",
 "noAutoVocab": true,
 "words": [],
 "understand": [
  {
   "p": "A short vowel followed by its matching letter becomes <strong>long</strong>, held about twice as long. The letter itself carries no vowel; it just stretches the sound."
  },
  {
   "table": {
    "headers": [
     "Pattern",
     "Sound",
     "Example"
    ],
    "ar": [
     2
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "fatḥa + ا",
      "ā (as in father)",
      "بَا"
     ],
     [
      "kasra + ي",
      "ī (as in see)",
      "بِي"
     ],
     [
      "ḍamma + و",
      "ū (as in moon)",
      "بُو"
     ]
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "بَابٌ",
     "en": "bābun: door"
    },
    {
     "ar": "نُورٌ",
     "en": "nūrun: light"
    },
    {
     "ar": "دِينٌ",
     "en": "dīnun: religion"
    },
    {
     "ar": "كِتَابٌ",
     "en": "kitābun: book"
    },
    {
     "ar": "سُوقٌ",
     "en": "sūqun: market"
    },
    {
     "ar": "كَبِيرٌ",
     "en": "kabīrun: big"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "rule": "Length changes meaning. <span class='ar'>كَتَبَ</span> kataba (he wrote) vs <span class='ar'>كَاتِبٌ</span> kātibun (writer). Read the stretch clearly."
  },
  {
   "tip": "و and ي are also consonants (w, y). When they have their own vowel or sukūn after a fatḥa, they're consonants: <span class='ar'>بَيْتٌ</span> bay-tun, <span class='ar'>يَوْمٌ</span> yaw-mun."
  }
 ],
